 "widgets": [
  {
   "type": "richTextWidget",
   "id": "78c398fe-9313-4a29-abf0-23e89b3abaf0",
   "name": "Rich Text (11)",
   "text": "<div style=\"height:100px; width:100%\">\n<h3><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><strong><span style=\"color:#bd672a\">Intersection Density</span></strong></span></h3>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Intersection density measures the number of three- and four-point intersections per hectare.</span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><strong><span style=\"color:#bd672a\">Why is this important? </span></strong></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Intersection density is a common measure of street connectivity. A higher density of street intersections creates pedestrian-friendly and transit supportive communities. As connectivity increases, travel distances decrease. This creates a more accessible network that supports complete community principles. </span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><strong><span style=\"color:#bd672a\">How is it being measured? </span></strong></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\">This indicator is calculated by adding the count of&nbsp; T-Intersections (three nodes)&nbsp;within a dissemination area (DA)&nbsp;multiplied&nbsp;by 0.67 to the count&nbsp;of Cross Intersection (four nodes) within a DA multiplied by 1.00 and dividing the result by the total area of the DA.</span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"color:#bd672a\"><strong>Data Source</strong></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><strong>Intersections:</strong> Single-Line Street Network* (non-boulevard), Region of Peel, 2016</span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"color:#bd672a\"><strong>Notes</strong></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\">A weighted intersection density calculation was created to account for the reduced connectivity associated with t-intersections (three nodes) compared to&nbsp;cross intersections (four nodes). Three-point intersections are weighted as 0.67 and four-point intersections were weighted as 1.00.</span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\">*Private roads, highways, freeways and ramps were excluded.</span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n</div>\n",
   "showLastUpdate": true,
   "noDataVerticalAlignment": "middle",
   "showCaptionWhenNoData": true,
   "showDescriptionWhenNoData": true
  },
